      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x-9} & =x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{5x-9})}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ 5x-9 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(2)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}2-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=2$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(5)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1$  are $x=2,\\,5.$",
